\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Example}
\title{A nonstable retained set need not inherit the action}
\date{2026-10-29}

\p{In the [alternating four-cycle](fdip-0003) with weights
#{(1,t,1,t)} and #{t,m>0}, consider the [half-turn](fdip-000J)
and the adjacent retained subset #{R=\{0,1\}}. The half-turn preserves
the full state, but sends #{R} to #{D=\{2,3\}}. No map #{T} of
retained fields can satisfy #{\pi_RP=T\pi_R}: the zero field
forces #{T(0)=0}, while #{\delta_2} has retained projection zero
and projects to #{(1,0)} after the half-turn. The action transports
the marginal to a different subset; it does not descend through this
fixed projection. This does not exclude accidental symmetries of an
individual two-site density. A general restricted action may also be
trivial, unlike the [opposite-pair swap](fdip-000K). Extra mass fields
or reference pairings would require their own preservation hypotheses.}

\p{The stable-subset requirement is the equivariance convention of
\citet{p. 58}{milne2025group}. The two-field witness proves failure of
factorization even through a nonlinear function. It differs from the
[fixed-label relabeling failure](fdip-003J): here the full
[half-turn symmetry](fdip-000J) is unchanged, and the chosen projection
fails to admit its action. The moving square of \ref{fdip-003E} remains
valid on #{\mathbb R^{pR}}. Over any nonzero commutative unital
coefficient ring #{k}, the same delta-field witness works on #{k^V};
over the zero ring all fields coincide.}
